@database "context_usage_tracker"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: context_usage_tracker.guide 1.0 (29.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Context & Cost Usage"
@{h1}Context & Cost Usage

What every LLM call costs: the plugin records each call's tokens, what
the prompt cache saved and wrote, the latency, the price, and how full the
model's context window was. The @{b}Context & Cost Usage@{ub} panel adds
it up per session -- sub-agents included -- per agent and per model, and
shows every call on its own. Other parts of the system read the same
records: the chat's @{tt}/costs@{utt} and @{tt}/context@{utt}, and the
plugins that compact a conversation when its context runs full.

The plugin gives the model no tool and changes nothing it sees; it only
counts.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Context & Cost Usage panel with the figures of a session"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The panel  " link panel}

For agents and developers
  @{"  What it records  " link hooks}
  @{"  Who reads the figures  " link readers}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The panel"
@{h1}The Context & Cost Usage panel

Open it from the launcher (@{b}Context@{ub}, "Context & Cost Usage"), or
from a session's info button, which opens it on that session.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Context & Cost Usage panel"}

@{h2}Which calls it counts

Two buttons in the toolbar choose the scope. @{b}Chat session@{ub} shows
the session open in the chat and switches with it; opened from a
session's info button it stays on that session instead -- the button then
names it, and @{b}Follow the chat@{ub} lets go of it. With no session open
it says so rather than counting every session. @{b}All sessions@{ub}
counts every call; only an admin may look at that (see below).

A session's calls include those of its sub-agents, at any depth: they run
in sessions of their own, but their request ids start with the request id
of the session that spawned them.

The figures look at the newest calls only -- 1000 by default, the
server's @{tt}max_history@{utt} -- per session, or over all sessions. The
database keeps more (see @{" Setting it up " link setup}). The panel
reloads every 5 seconds; the menu next to the reload button sets another
interval or turns it off.

@{h2}The figures at the top

@{table}
Figure | Shows
Total cost | the sum of every priced call, in cents; ~ in front when some prices are estimates. Below it how many calls were billed and how many estimated
Calls | how many calls the figures cover
Output tokens | the tokens the model wrote; below it the prompt tokens
Cached | prompt tokens read from the provider's cache, and their share of the prompt
Cache writes | prompt tokens written into the cache
Context now | prompt and output tokens of the last call that carried a conversation, and their share of that model's window; in a session's view @{i}stale@{ui} when the conversation was compacted since
@{body}

A price is either billed -- the provider reported it with the answer --
or estimated from @{tt}config/llm_pricing.yaml@{utt}. An estimate carries
a ~ and the warning colour, a billed price is green; a call priced neither
way has no price and counts as none, not as zero.

@{h2}The tabs

@{bullet}@{b}Overview@{ub}: a chart of the newest 60 calls -- total,
prompt, output and cached tokens on the left scale, the cost in cents on
the right -- for all agents or the one chosen above it. A line clicked in
the legend is hidden and stays hidden. Below it the context tokens and
the share of the window used: now, lowest, highest and average. Calls
without a conversation (see @{" What it records " link hooks}) are left
out of these two rows.
@{bullet}@{b}Agents@{ub}: per agent the calls, token sums, cached tokens
with their share of the prompt, cache writes, total and average cost,
average latency, the largest call and the time of the last. Most
expensive first. Under @{b}All sessions@{ub} these are all-time totals,
kept apart from the calls, so they count further back than the other
figures.
@{bullet}@{b}LLMs@{ub}: calls, token sums, cached share and cost per model,
with the average and the 95th percentile latency. Most used first.
@{bullet}@{b}Calls@{ub}: the newest 50, 100, 250 or 500 calls, newest
first, for all agents or one: time, agent, session and request, model,
tokens, share of the window, latency and cost. In a session's view, a
call of a sub-agent is marked ↳. A row -- click, Enter or Space -- opens the call with everything
recorded about it, including the estimated size of the tool definitions
and the number of messages sent.

A click on a column head sorts a table by it, a second click the other
way; the order holds through the reloads.

@{h2}Clearing

The bin in the toolbar deletes every recorded call and every agent's
totals, of every session, after asking. Only an admin may; for anyone
else the server refuses and the panel shows why.

@{h2}Who sees what

With authentication on, a user sees the figures of her own sessions. A
session of another user shows nothing, as if it had no calls.
@{b}All sessions@{ub} and clearing are for admins; for anyone else the
panel shows the server's refusal instead of the figures. With
authentication off, whoever uses the instance sees everything.
@endnode

@node hooks "What it records"
@{h1}What it records

Two hooks record the calls. Both are on for every agent as soon as the
plugin is enabled; neither has settings.

@{h2}track_usage -- the calls of an agent

Runs after each LLM call an agent makes (@{tt}post_llm_call@{utt}) and
stores one row:

@{table}
Field | From
agent, session, request id | the agent that made the call
model | the client that served it -- a model chosen in the chat counts, not the agent's default
prompt, output, cached, cache-write tokens | the provider's usage, read the same way for every provider
total tokens | the provider's total, else prompt plus output
context window | the client that served the call, else the agent's configured model
share of the window | total tokens divided by the window
tool definitions | an estimate of the tokens the tool schemas took
messages | how many messages were sent
latency | the wall-clock time of the answer
cost | billed by the provider, else estimated; a batch call at the batch price
@{body}

A call whose answer carries no usage is not recorded. A failure while
recording is logged and never fails the call.

To leave out one agent:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    overrides:
      context_usage_tracker.track_usage:
        enabled: false
@{body}

@{h2}track_non_agent_usage -- calls without an agent

Some clients call a provider with no agent around them: the decisions
client, and speech synthesis. This hook (@{tt}post_llm_response@{utt})
records exactly those -- a call made by an agent is left to the hook
above, so nothing is counted twice. The row is booked under the name the
call gives, else the provider's name, with a context window of 0: such a
call carries no conversation, so it counts as spend but never as a
context reading.

It records only what the provider measured. A call reported without a
usage -- a failure that was never billed, a retry, a synthesis from a
provider that returns audio and no token count -- leaves no row. A call
billed and then refused by the client is booked: it was spent.

@{h2}Stale after a compaction

When a plugin that compacts the conversation rewrites it, it marks the
session's last reading @{i}stale@{ui}: the tokens counted describe a
conversation that no longer exists. The next call of the conversation
clears the mark; a call without a context does not.
@endnode

@node readers "Who reads the figures"
@{h1}Who reads the figures

The records are the one place that sees every call of the process,
sub-agents' included, so several parts of the system ask them. They find
the plugin by its instance name, @{tt}context_usage_tracker@{utt}: an
instance under another name records, but nobody else reads it.

@{bullet}The chat's @{tt}/costs@{utt} adds up the calls, tokens, cache
share and cost of the session and its sub-agents; @{tt}/context@{utt}
shows what the provider counted on the last call next to the estimate of
what the conversation holds now.
@{bullet}The context engineer and the context summarizer take the larger
of the last call's prompt tokens and their own estimate as the size of
the context, and mark
the session stale after they compacted it.
@{bullet}The panel of the sub-agent manager shows each sub-agent's
context from the same records.

@{h2}The web API

Under @{tt}/plugins/context_usage_tracker/@{utt}:

@{table}
Request | Answers
GET usage?session_id= | @{tt}latest@{utt} (the last call with a conversation; with @{tt}is_stale@{utt} when a session is asked for), @{tt}agents@{utt} (per agent), @{tt}statistics@{utt} (timespan, context series, totals)
GET history?session_id=&agent_id=&last_n= | @{tt}history@{utt}: the calls, oldest first; without last_n every call the figures cover
POST clear | deletes everything; @{tt}{"status": "cleared"}@{utt}
GET / | the panel
@{body}

Without @{tt}session_id@{utt} the answers cover every session; only an
admin may ask so, anyone else gets 403. Another user's session answers
empty. Without a session store in the process the answer is 503.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

Nothing. The plugin has no tool, and both hooks only read the call after
it happened: they add no message, change no prompt and leave the prompt
cache as it was.

The cost is time, and small. The row is written off the event loop, so
other sessions keep running; this call waits for it, at most the hook's
5 seconds, after which it goes on and the write finishes in the
background.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}A synthesis from a provider that reports no token usage costs
money but leaves no row here.
@{bullet}The rows carry no owner. A session id that another user's
deleted session had, used again, brings that user's old calls along.
@{bullet}Old calls are pruned only by a process that has written 500
calls since it started; a process that never gets there prunes nothing.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    context_usage_tracker:
      type: context_usage_tracker
      enabled: true
@{body}

Keep the instance name @{tt}context_usage_tracker@{utt}; the chat and
the context plugins look for it by that name. No agent needs an allowlist
line -- there is no tool -- and both hooks run without being switched on.

@{h2}Server settings

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
storage_path | context_usage_tracker.json in the data directory | names the store; the database is usage.db in a folder of the same name without the suffix
max_history | 1000 | how many of the newest calls the figures and the panel look at
@{body}

With the defaults the calls are in
@{tt}data/context_usage_tracker/usage.db@{utt}, an SQLite database that
the API, the command line and workers write at the same time. Calls older
than 90 days are pruned, and at most 500,000 are kept; the agents' totals
are kept apart and are not pruned. A @{tt}context_usage_tracker.json@{utt}
from an older version at @{tt}storage_path@{utt} is imported once, the
first time the database is opened, and left in place.
@endnode
